// The Earth map type (`MapType::Earth`): the real world's land, relief and
// great rivers, sampled at any map size. Positions are (longitude, latitude)
// in degrees, east and north positive; the map runs from 180° W at its west
// edge and its rows span the full 90° N to 90° S, so the climate model's
// latitudes are the real ones.
//
// `land` and `masses`: 360 rows of 720 half-degree cells, north to south,
// west to east from 180° W, each row run-length encoded (a symbol, then how
// many cells it repeats for when more than one). `land` is the share of land
// in each cell, '.' (sea) or 'a' (a ninth) to 'i' (all land), or 'L' where a
// lake covers most of it. `masses` says which landmass (continent or larger
// island) covers most of the cell, one symbol each ('.' none): the generator
// keeps different landmasses apart at every size, so Britain stays an
// island. Both are rasterised from Natural Earth 1:50m land and lakes
// (public domain) by `data/tools/earth_mask.py`, which rewrites them in place.
//
// `straits`: straits and narrow seas within one landmass (Gibraltar, the
// Bosporus, the Red Sea) kept open at every size, as paths of sea tiles.
//
// `ranges`: mountain ranges and uplands as paths with a half-width (degrees).
// `Mountains` raise peaks along the path and foothills beside it; `Hills`
// raise only hills.
//
// `rivers`: great rivers from source to mouth. Smaller rivers are added by
// the generator.
//
// `climate`: regional corrections to the generator's climate model, for what
// latitude and winds alone don't give (warm and cold ocean currents,
// monsoons): ellipses of (half-width, half-height) degrees that add to the
// temperature (°C) and moisture (0..1) at their centre, fading to nothing at
// their rim.
//
// Every civ's historical homeland (`home` in civs.ron) is used by the "True
// Start Locations" setting on this map.
